// The leaderboard's TIER palette — which colour a Daily board wears.
//
// David ruled this on 2026-08-31 and it REVERSES a deliberate earlier decision,
// so the reasoning on both sides is worth keeping:
//
//   "cyan should replace the purple for challenge actually, everywhere …
//    including battles, dailys, leaderboards … easy green, challenge cyan, and
//    expert gold"
//
// and then, on the collision that created:
//
//   "tier colours win; the per-board colour language gives way"
//
// WHAT GAVE WAY. `dailyModeTabs.js` used to argue — correctly, at the time —
// that the mode row should wear ONE colour, because the sub-tab row beneath it
// already carried a colour per BOARD (Global cyan · Friends green · Equations
// magenta · National violet) and two colour languages stacked on one screen
// would leave neither readable. What broke that argument is that Challenge MOVED
// ONTO cyan. The mode row's single shared accent was the Daily's cyan, so from
// 08-31 an Expert board was labelled in Challenge's colour — measured, not
// assumed: the selected pill rendered rgb(31,240,255) on all three modes.
//
// The resolution is the one David picked of the three on the table: the Daily
// surface has ONE colour language and it is the tier. A board's identity is
// carried by its label and its icon (Globe · Users · Percent · Flag), which are
// already there; a tier's identity had nothing else to carry it.
//
// WHY A JS MAP AND NOT `var(--u37-mode-accent)`. Both exist and both are used:
// `DailyTab` sets `data-mode` on the board root so anything painting from the
// token slot follows for free. But the boards pass explicit accent STRINGS down
// into `SubTabBar` and `LeaderboardRow` props, and a prop carrying
// `var(--u37-mode-accent)` is the same string for all three tiers — a test could
// not tell an Expert board from an Easy one, and neither could a reviewer
// reading a diff. So the map is explicit, and `modeAccent.test.js` pins it
// against the very `[data-mode]` rules in tokens.css that the slot resolves,
// which is the same guard `badgeModeTokens.test.js` puts on `badgeVisuals`.

import { modeOf } from '@/game-modes';

/**
 * Difficulty tier → the CSS custom property a board paints with.
 *
 * These are the token NAMES, not literals, so a palette change in tokens.css
 * reaches every leaderboard surface without touching this file.
 */
export const TIER_ACCENTS = Object.freeze({
  easy: 'var(--u37-green)',
  challenge: 'var(--u37-cyan)',
  expert: 'var(--u37-gold)',
});

/**
 * The accent for a daily wire key.
 *
 * `modeOf` coerces anything unrecognised to 'easy'. That is the safe direction
 * for RENDERING — a stale mode string yields the gentlest colour rather than
 * `undefined`, which would collapse an inline style to no colour at all and take
 * the pill's border and glow with it.
 *
 * @param {string} key a daily wire key ('daily_easy' | 'daily' | 'daily_expert')
 * @returns {string} a `var(--u37-…)` reference
 */
export const dailyModeAccent = (key) => TIER_ACCENTS[modeOf(key)];
