// Puzzle Pieces — WHAT EARNS ONE, and how many.
//
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// THE RULE (David, 2026-09-04) — the whole of it, in three lines
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//   • 1 Piece — a PERFECT solve on the CHALLENGE Daily. Unchanged; this is the
//     rule that has always paid, and every existing balance was earned under it.
//   • 2 Pieces — the EXPERT Daily completed in UNDER 37 SECONDS. New.
//   • So at most 3 in a day, and that ceiling is arithmetic, not a clamp.
//
// Note the SHAPE of the second rule, because it is easy to get wrong: an Expert
// solve is perfect by the mode's own entry gate (`requiresPerfectSolve`), so
// perfection is not what distinguishes a paying Expert solve from a non-paying
// one. TIME is. 37 is the brand number and it lives in ONE named constant here —
// a literal 37 sprinkled through the solve path is how one rule quietly becomes
// three slightly different rules.
//
// Easy earns nothing, and cannot: Easy deals no coloured dice, so a perfect solve
// is arithmetically unreachable (`hasPerfectConcept`) and there is no Expert-style
// time clause for it.
//
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// WHERE THIS RULE IS ENFORCED — and why the same constants appear twice
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// The AUTHORITATIVE award is server-side, in
// `supabase/functions/_shared/puzzlePointsLedger.ts`, because Pieces are currency
// and a client may not mint currency. This module is the CLIENT's copy of the
// same rule, used for two things a server round-trip cannot serve:
//   1. the provisional point total the win celebration reads at the instant of a
//      solve (`game-flow/solveCompletion.js` → `provisionalPoints`), before the
//      authoritative total lands, and
//   2. the COPY every surface shows for "how do I earn one of these"
//      (`PUZZLE_PIECE_RULES` / `describePuzzlePieceRule`), so no screen can
//      hard-code a subtitle the rule has since outgrown.
//
// The edge runtime cannot import from `src/`, so the constants genuinely exist in
// two files. That duplication is PINNED, not tolerated: `puzzlePoints.test.js`
// reads the ledger's TypeScript source and fails if the two ever disagree.
//
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// IDEMPOTENCE — the property that matters most here
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// `countPuzzlePieces` counts by DISTINCT (tier, date). A Daily is once per day
// per mode, so one date can pay each rule at most once no matter how many rows
// describe it — a retry, a double-tap, a duplicate row predating the
// UNIQUE(user_id, date, mode) constraint, or the client's own two-writer submit
// flow all produce the same number. Running the count ten times in a row returns
// what it returned the first time. That is what stops a player banking the Expert
// pair twice by any route, and it is asserted directly in the tests.

import { isPerfectSolve } from '@/equation-engine';
import { isDaily, isKnownModeKey, modeOf } from '@/game-modes';

/**
 * A solve on the Expert Daily pays only if it came in STRICTLY under this many
 * seconds. 37 is the brand number, and the comparison is `<`, not `<=` — 37.0
 * exactly does not pay.
 */
export const EXPERT_PIECE_SECONDS = 37;

/** Pieces for a perfect Challenge Daily. The original rule; do not change lightly. */
export const PIECES_PER_PERFECT_CHALLENGE = 1;

/** Pieces for an Expert Daily under {@link EXPERT_PIECE_SECONDS}. */
export const PIECES_PER_FAST_EXPERT = 2;

/**
 * The most a player can earn in one ET day. DERIVED, never hand-written: the two
 * rules are the only ways to earn, each pays at most once per date, and a date
 * can hold one Challenge Daily and one Expert Daily. If a third rule is ever
 * added this number moves on its own.
 */
export const MAX_PIECES_PER_DAY = PIECES_PER_PERFECT_CHALLENGE + PIECES_PER_FAST_EXPERT;

/**
 * The rule table, as data — the ONE place a surface should read to describe how
 * Pieces are earned. Statistics' Puzzle Pieces tile and any future help copy read
 * this instead of hard-coding "Complete Daily Challenge with a Perfect Solve",
 * which was true before 2026-09-04 and is now half the story.
 *
 * `label` is the short phrase for a tile; `earnedBy` is the same thing as a
 * complete prepositional phrase, carrying its own article. Two fields rather
 * than one because a caller composing "N for a {label}" produces "2 for a Expert
 * Daily" — the article cannot be generated, so each rule states its own.
 *
 * @type {ReadonlyArray<{mode: string, tier: string, pieces: number,
 *   label: string, earnedBy: string}>}
 */
export const PUZZLE_PIECE_RULES = Object.freeze([
  Object.freeze({
    mode: 'daily',
    tier: 'challenge',
    pieces: PIECES_PER_PERFECT_CHALLENGE,
    label: 'Perfect Challenge Daily',
    earnedBy: 'for a perfect solve on the Challenge Daily',
  }),
  Object.freeze({
    mode: 'daily_expert',
    tier: 'expert',
    pieces: PIECES_PER_FAST_EXPERT,
    label: `Expert Daily under ${EXPERT_PIECE_SECONDS}s`,
    earnedBy: `for the Expert Daily in under ${EXPERT_PIECE_SECONDS} seconds`,
  }),
]);

/**
 * One line of copy describing every way to earn a Piece, generated from
 * {@link PUZZLE_PIECE_RULES} so it can never fall behind the rule.
 *
 * @returns {string} "1 Piece for a perfect solve on the Challenge Daily ·
 *   2 Pieces for the Expert Daily in under 37 seconds"
 */
/**
 * What a surface should tell a player they earn a Piece FOR, in one mode.
 *
 * ⚠️ THE NAME IS THE CONTRACT. `components/stats/perModeTiles.js` has asked
 * `@/game-mechanics` for an export called exactly `PUZZLE_PIECE_RULE_TEXT` since
 * the Puzzle-Pieces tile was written, falling back to the pre-2026-09-04 wording
 * when it is absent. That export never existed — the module offered
 * `describePuzzlePieceRule` instead — so the two halves of a deliberate seam
 * never met, and EVERY tile silently rendered the legacy line:
 *
 *     "Complete a Daily with a Perfect Solve"
 *
 * which is now half the story, and the wrong half on the mode the $37 giveaway
 * is won on: Expert earns its Pieces for SPEED, not for perfection (an Expert
 * solve is perfect by the mode's own entry gate).
 *
 * A function of the mode key, because the tile is per-mode and the rule now
 * differs by tier — which is the shape `perModeTiles.js` already handles.
 *
 * @param {string} modeKey a wire key from the game-modes vocabulary
 * @returns {string} the caption for that mode's tile
 */
export function PUZZLE_PIECE_RULE_TEXT(modeKey) {
  const tier = modeOf(modeKey);
  const rule = PUZZLE_PIECE_RULES.find((r) => r.tier === tier);
  // Easy earns no Pieces at all, and saying nothing would leave the tile with a
  // caption describing a rule that cannot apply to the mode it sits under.
  if (!rule) return 'No Pieces from this mode';
  return `${rule.pieces} ${rule.pieces === 1 ? 'Piece' : 'Pieces'} ${rule.earnedBy}`;
}

export function describePuzzlePieceRule() {
  return PUZZLE_PIECE_RULES
    .map((r) => `${r.pieces} ${r.pieces === 1 ? 'Piece' : 'Pieces'} ${r.earnedBy}`)
    .join(' · ');
}

/**
 * The SHORT per-mode rule line for modals and cards (copy-condense, 2026-10-18).
 * Null where the mode pays nothing — Easy, every practice mode, and Blitz — so
 * a caller can hide its row instead of promising a Piece the game never awards.
 *
 * @param {string} modeKey a wire key from the game-modes vocabulary
 * @returns {string|null} "Perfect solve = 1 Puzzle Piece" /
 *   "Under 37 seconds = 2 Puzzle Pieces" / null
 */
export function PUZZLE_PIECE_RULE_SHORT(modeKey) {
  if (!isKnownModeKey(modeKey) || !isDaily(modeKey)) return null;
  const rule = PUZZLE_PIECE_RULES.find((r) => r.tier === modeOf(modeKey));
  if (!rule) return null;
  const unit = rule.pieces === 1 ? 'Puzzle Piece' : 'Puzzle Pieces';
  return rule.tier === 'expert'
    ? `Under ${EXPERT_PIECE_SECONDS} seconds = ${rule.pieces} ${unit}`
    : `Perfect solve = ${rule.pieces} ${unit}`;
}

/**
 * Every way to earn a Piece, in one line, for legends and help copy:
 * "Perfect Challenge Daily = 1 · Expert Daily under 37s = 2".
 */
export const PUZZLE_PIECE_RULE_SUMMARY = PUZZLE_PIECE_RULES
  .map((r) => `${r.label} = ${r.pieces}`)
  .join(' · ');

/** A recorded (finite, positive) solve time. 0 and null both mean NOT RECORDED. */
const isRecordedTime = (seconds) =>
  typeof seconds === 'number' && Number.isFinite(seconds) && seconds > 0;

/**
 * How many Pieces ONE solve is worth. The rule, as a pure function.
 *
 * `isPerfect` is required on the Expert side too, even though Expert's entry gate
 * already guarantees it. That is not redundant defence: this predicate is also
 * run over STORED rows, and a legacy or hand-written row that never passed the
 * referee must not be able to mint currency merely by carrying a small number in
 * `solve_time_seconds`.
 *
 * @param {{mode?: string, isPerfect?: boolean, solveTimeSeconds?: number|null}} [solve]
 * @returns {number} 0, {@link PIECES_PER_PERFECT_CHALLENGE}, or
 *   {@link PIECES_PER_FAST_EXPERT}.
 */
export function piecesForSolve({ mode, isPerfect, solveTimeSeconds } = {}) {
  // Untrusted input: an unrecognised mode earns nothing rather than coercing to
  // a tier that might pay (the predicates normalize, so `modeOf('nonsense')` is
  // 'easy' — harmless today, but the guard keeps that true by construction).
  if (!isKnownModeKey(mode) || !isDaily(mode)) return 0;
  if (isPerfect !== true) return 0;

  const tier = modeOf(mode);
  if (tier === 'challenge') return PIECES_PER_PERFECT_CHALLENGE;
  if (tier === 'expert') {
    if (!isRecordedTime(solveTimeSeconds)) return 0;
    return solveTimeSeconds < EXPERT_PIECE_SECONDS ? PIECES_PER_FAST_EXPERT : 0;
  }
  return 0;
}

/**
 * Total Pieces earned across a set of solve rows — IDEMPOTENT by construction.
 *
 * Rows are collapsed by (tier, date) and the best value for that key wins, so
 * duplicate rows for one date in one mode pay exactly once. Feeding the same rows
 * in twice returns the same number.
 *
 * @param {ReadonlyArray<{date?: string, mode?: string, isPerfect?: boolean,
 *   solveTimeSeconds?: number|null}>} [rows]
 * @returns {number}
 */
export function countPuzzlePieces(rows) {
  if (!Array.isArray(rows)) return 0;

  /** @type {Map<string, number>} */
  const byTierAndDate = new Map();
  for (const row of rows) {
    if (!row || typeof row.date !== 'string' || !row.date) continue;
    const pieces = piecesForSolve(row);
    if (pieces === 0) continue;
    const key = `${modeOf(row.mode)}:${row.date}`;
    byTierAndDate.set(key, Math.max(byTierAndDate.get(key) || 0, pieces));
  }

  let total = 0;
  for (const pieces of byTierAndDate.values()) total += pieces;
  return total;
}

// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// LEGACY — the game_stats-blob derivation
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

/**
 * True when all three coloured power dice were used correctly. Power-dice
 * correctness lives in the engine (one read path, positional); see ADR-0002.
 */
function allThreeCorrect(equation, usedDiceIndices, diceColors) {
  if (!equation || !usedDiceIndices || !diceColors) return false;

  const coloredCount = diceColors.filter(c => c && c !== 'white').length;
  if (coloredCount !== 3) return false;

  return isPerfectSolve(equation, usedDiceIndices, diceColors);
}

/**
 * Count Pieces from the `dailyChallengeSolutions` array in the game_stats blob.
 *
 * ⚠️ This is the PRE-2026-09-04 rule and it knows nothing about mode, date or
 * time: it counts one Piece per perfect solution in the array. It survives
 * because that array is Challenge-Daily-only by construction (the controller
 * rebuilds it from `mode = 'daily'` rows), so it still answers the narrow
 * question "how many perfect Challenge Dailies" correctly — but it CANNOT see
 * the Expert rule and will under-report a real balance. Anything wanting the
 * current earning rule uses {@link countPuzzlePieces}; anything wanting a
 * player's actual balance reads the server-owned `profiles.puzzle_points`.
 */
export function calculatePuzzlePoints(dailyChallengeSolutions) {
  if (!dailyChallengeSolutions || !Array.isArray(dailyChallengeSolutions)) {
    return 0;
  }

  let points = 0;

  for (const solution of dailyChallengeSolutions) {
    const { equation, usedDiceIndices, diceColors } = solution;
    if (equation && usedDiceIndices && diceColors) {
      if (allThreeCorrect(equation, usedDiceIndices, diceColors)) {
        points++;
      }
    }
  }

  return points;
}
