// announcedAwards — the ONE record of what this player has already been told.
//
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// THE PROBLEM
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// There are six award families (streak · puzzle · battle · perfect · time ·
// registry badge), and until now only ONE of them remembered anything. Registry
// badges persist `announcedBadges`; the other five were diffed against an
// in-memory pre-solve snapshot inside a single callback and `onComplete` wrote
// nothing at all.
//
// That is fine while every celebration is guaranteed to play the moment it is
// earned — and it is not guaranteed. A queue is lost whenever the app is closed
// mid-sequence, whenever the player navigates away, and (by design) whenever the
// settle pass lands too late to replace a sequence already on screen. There was
// no record that could tell us afterwards, so those moments were gone for good.
//
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// THE RECORD
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// One versioned localStorage key PER ACCOUNT holding `{ identity: rank }` — the HIGHEST rung
// announced for each award identity, across every family:
//
//     { "streak:37": 0, "perfect:7": 2, "badge:gladiator": 3, "puzzle:gold": 0 }
//
//   • IDENTITY is the ladder — `${kind}:${value}` for the five stat families,
//     `badge:${badgeId}` for the registry, `podium:${date}:${mode}` for a podium
//     placement. Never a display string: names and copy change, identities must
//     not.
//   • RANK is how far up that ladder we have gone. `0` for a once-ever award
//     (you cannot reach a 37-day streak for the second time — `reachedMilestones`
//     is a set). The badge TIER for a laddered badge. The repeat COUNT for a
//     perfect-streak milestone, which genuinely is repeatable:
//     `perfectStreakMilestones[m]` is a counter, incremented every time the
//     streak hits `m` again.
//
// An award is unannounced iff `rank > held`, with an absent identity held at -1
// so that rank 0 still counts as new. That single comparison is the whole rule,
// and it is why a repeatable award and a once-ever award can share one record.
//
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// WHY THE WRITE HAPPENS ON DRAIN, NEVER ON PLAN
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Recording at plan time marks a moment announced that the player may never see —
// they close the app on screen 1 of 3 and the other two are gone forever. So the
// record is written when a queue DRAINS, which is the only instant at which
// "they have been told" is actually true. This is the same reasoning that made
// `mergeAnnounced` exist rather than persisting `planBadgeAnnouncement`'s
// `nextSnapshot` wholesale — see badgeAnnouncements.js.
//
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// ONE RECORD PER ACCOUNT, NOT PER DEVICE (2026-10-18)
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// The record is stored under `announcedAwards.v1:<userId>`. It used to be one
// device-wide key, so whoever signed in next — a second account, or the account
// a guest signed into — was diffed against SOMEONE ELSE'S history: their badges
// read as already announced and never popped (docs/bugs.md 2026-10-18). David's
// ruling: start fresh per account.
//
//   • The old unkeyed `announcedAwards.v1` and the legacy `announcedBadges` are
//     IGNORED, never inherited — nothing in them says whose they were. They are
//     not deleted either: a CapGo rollback to a build that still reads them gets
//     a stale record rather than a first-run summary for everyone.
//   • No user id (the player is still loading) reads as a first run with an
//     empty record and NEVER writes, so a shared key can never be re-created.
//   • Accepted cost: each account sees its one retroactive "badge case" summary
//     once per device.
//
// (`lastSeenBadges` has no writer left — the last one went with the satellite
// modal. The only remaining mention is a comment.)

import { ANNOUNCED_BADGES_KEY } from './badgeAnnouncements';
import { stampEarnedBadges } from './badgeEarnedStamps';

/**
 * The record's key PREFIX; the real key is `announcedAwardsKey(userId)`. The
 * VERSION IS IN THE NAME on purpose: a future shape change ships as `v2` plus a
 * v1→v2 absorb in `readAnnouncedAwards`, which makes a migration additive and a
 * rollback survivable. The bare prefix is the pre-2026-10-18 device-wide key,
 * now ignored.
 */
export const ANNOUNCED_AWARDS_KEY = 'announcedAwards.v1';

/**
 * This account's record key, or null when there is no account to key it by.
 * @param {string|null|undefined} userId
 * @returns {string|null}
 */
export function announcedAwardsKey(userId) {
  return typeof userId === 'string' && userId ? `${ANNOUNCED_AWARDS_KEY}:${userId}` : null;
}

/** The pre-unification, device-wide badge record. Ignored since 2026-10-18; never written. */
export { ANNOUNCED_BADGES_KEY };

const BADGE_PREFIX = 'badge:';

/**
 * Reserved record key: has the RETROACTIVE badge summary already happened?
 *
 * 🔴 THE TRAP THIS EXISTS TO CLOSE. `isFirstRun` means "no record of any kind",
 * and it governs two independent questions that used to be the same question:
 *
 *   1. have the five STAT families ever been recorded?  (new in slice 6)
 *   2. has the badge family had its one retroactive summary?  (much older)
 *
 * The moment the Home catch-up seeds (1), a record exists — so (2) would answer
 * "not a first run" and `planBadgeAnnouncement` would route every history-earned
 * badge down the LIVE path. A long-time player would open the app to a burst of
 * pops for badges earned months ago, and the "you've unlocked N badges" summary
 * would be lost. That is precisely the failure the summary exists to prevent,
 * re-introduced by a sibling feature.
 *
 * `BADGE_SYSTEM_LAUNCH_DATE` is 2026-09-05, so essentially EVERY player is in
 * the badges' first-run branch right now. This is not a corner case.
 *
 * The `@` prefix is unreachable by `awardIdentity`, whose keys are always
 * `${kind}:${…}` over a closed set of kinds — so no award can ever collide with
 * it, and `badgeSnapshotFromRecord` skips it for free (it is not `badge:`).
 */
export const BADGE_RETRO_KEY = '@badgeRetroShown';

/**
 * Reserved record key: have the five STAT families been seeded yet?
 *
 * 🔴 THE SECOND HALF OF THE SAME TRAP, and it shipped before it was caught.
 * `BADGE_RETRO_KEY` split "have the badges had their summary?" out of
 * `isFirstRun`. But `isFirstRun` was ALSO still answering "have the stat
 * families ever been recorded?" — and it is false for anyone holding the legacy
 * `announcedBadges` key, i.e. anyone who has ever opened the Stats page.
 *
 * So the most engaged players — precisely the ones with the largest backlog —
 * skipped the one-summary branch and got the ordinary catch-up: three
 * individual award screens plus a summary, for milestones earned months ago.
 * That is the slideshow the ruling exists to prevent, delivered to exactly the
 * wrong audience. Measured before the fix: a player with four streak
 * milestones, two perfect streaks, a sub-minute solve and both medal ladders
 * got **10 awards across 4 screens**.
 *
 * The lesson, twice over: `isFirstRun` means "no record of ANY kind", which is
 * a fact about STORAGE. Every question about a FAMILY needs its own marker.
 */
export const STATS_SEEDED_KEY = '@statsSeeded';

/**
 * The stable ladder identity an award sits on, or `null` if it has none.
 *
 * `null` is a caller bug (every award carries a kind and a value), and the two
 * consumers treat it in opposite directions on purpose: `filterUnannounced`
 * lets it through (never silently eat a celebration) while
 * `mergeAnnouncedAwards` drops it (never write a key we cannot match again).
 *
 * @param {{kind?: string, value?: any, badgeId?: string, date?: string, mode?: string}} [award]
 * @returns {string|null}
 */
export function awardIdentity(award) {
  if (!award || typeof award !== 'object') return null;
  const kind = award.kind;
  if (!kind || typeof kind !== 'string') return null;

  if (kind === 'badge') {
    const badgeId = award.badgeId ?? award.value;
    return badgeId ? `${BADGE_PREFIX}${badgeId}` : null;
  }

  // A podium is per (date, mode): under three Dailies there are three podiums a
  // day and a player can legitimately place on more than one of them.
  if (kind === 'podium') {
    return award.date && award.mode ? `podium:${award.date}:${award.mode}` : null;
  }

  const { value } = award;
  if (value === undefined || value === null || value === '') return null;
  return `${kind}:${value}`;
}

/**
 * How far up its ladder this award sits. See the header: 0 for once-ever, the
 * tier for a badge, the repeat count for a perfect-streak milestone.
 *
 * ⚠️ `perfect` floors at 1, not 0. Its count is 1-based (`perfectStreakMilestones[m]`
 * is incremented TO 1 the first time), so a perfect award that reaches us
 * without a count — an older call site, a hand-built award in a test — must not
 * record as rank 0 and then let the genuine first announcement re-fire.
 *
 * @param {{kind?: string, tier?: number, count?: number}} [award]
 * @returns {number}
 */
export function awardRank(award) {
  if (!award || typeof award !== 'object') return 0;
  if (award.kind === 'badge') {
    return Number.isFinite(award.tier) ? Math.max(/** @type {number} */ (award.tier), 0) : 0;
  }
  if (award.kind === 'perfect') {
    return Number.isFinite(award.count) ? Math.max(/** @type {number} */ (award.count), 1) : 1;
  }
  return 0;
}

/**
 * Has this exact rung already been announced?
 * @param {Record<string, number>|null} [record]
 * @param {object} [award]
 * @returns {boolean}
 */
export function isAnnounced(record, award) {
  const identity = awardIdentity(award);
  if (!identity) return false;
  const held = record && Number.isFinite(record[identity]) ? record[identity] : -1;
  return held >= awardRank(award);
}

/**
 * The awards in this list the player has not been told about yet, in order.
 * @param {Array} [awards]
 * @param {Record<string, number>|null} [record]
 * @returns {Array}
 */
export function filterUnannounced(awards, record) {
  if (!Array.isArray(awards)) return [];
  return awards.filter((award) => !isAnnounced(record, award));
}

/**
 * Fold announced awards into the record. Ranks only ever move UP, so a lower
 * rung arriving late — a stale settle, a second surface racing the first — can
 * never un-announce a higher one.
 *
 * @param {Record<string, number>|null} [record]
 * @param {Array} [awards]
 * @returns {Record<string, number>}
 */
export function mergeAnnouncedAwards(record, awards = []) {
  /** @type {Record<string, number>} */
  const next = { ...(record || {}) };
  for (const award of awards || []) {
    const identity = awardIdentity(award);
    if (!identity) continue;
    const rank = awardRank(award);
    const held = Number.isFinite(next[identity]) ? next[identity] : -1;
    next[identity] = Math.max(held, rank);
  }
  return next;
}

/**
 * `{badgeId: tier}` → the award shapes the record understands.
 *
 * One conversion, three callers: absorbing the legacy key, letting the stats
 * page persist `planBadgeAnnouncement().nextSnapshot`, and any future surface
 * that holds a badge snapshot rather than an award list. Having exactly one is
 * what keeps the badge half of the record round-trippable.
 *
 * @param {Record<string, number>|null} [snapshot]
 * @returns {Array<{kind: 'badge', badgeId: string, tier: number}>}
 */
export function badgeSnapshotAwards(snapshot) {
  return Object.entries(snapshot || {})
    .filter(([badgeId]) => Boolean(badgeId))
    .map(([badgeId, tier]) => ({
      kind: /** @type {'badge'} */ ('badge'),
      badgeId,
      tier: Number.isFinite(tier) ? Math.max(/** @type {number} */ (tier), 0) : 0,
    }));
}

/**
 * The badge half of the record, back in the `{badgeId: tier}` shape
 * `planBadgeAnnouncement` diffs against.
 *
 * ⚠️ It must keep ids the catalog no longer has. That is not sloppiness — it is
 * how `planBadgeAnnouncement` detects a catalog RE-CUT (a stored id with no
 * badge behind it) and routes the resulting tier lifts into the one retroactive
 * summary instead of a burst of pops. Filtering to known ids here would silently
 * re-arm that storm.
 *
 * @param {Record<string, number>|null} [record]
 * @returns {Record<string, number>}
 */
export function badgeSnapshotFromRecord(record) {
  /** @type {Record<string, number>} */
  const snapshot = {};
  for (const [identity, rank] of Object.entries(record || {})) {
    if (!identity.startsWith(BADGE_PREFIX)) continue;
    const badgeId = identity.slice(BADGE_PREFIX.length);
    if (!badgeId) continue;
    snapshot[badgeId] = Number.isFinite(rank) ? Math.max(rank, 0) : 0;
  }
  return snapshot;
}

/**
 * Read this account's record.
 *
 * `isFirstRun` means this account has NEVER had a record on this device. It is
 * a different thing from an empty record, and the catch-up
 * path needs the distinction: a first run must SEED silently, because the five
 * stat families have been announcing live since long before this record existed
 * and a player's whole history is therefore already-announced, not a backlog.
 *
 * ⚠️ A storage failure reports `isFirstRun: true` with an empty record, and that
 * asymmetry is deliberate. Empty suppresses nothing, so a genuinely-new award
 * still gets its live moment; first-run makes the catch-up path seed rather than
 * announce. A dead localStorage therefore costs catch-up and never costs the
 * moment the player just earned. No user id reads the same way, for the same
 * reason.
 *
 * @param {string|null|undefined} userId whose record — see the header
 * @returns {{record: Record<string, number>, isFirstRun: boolean}}
 */
export function readAnnouncedAwards(userId) {
  const key = announcedAwardsKey(userId);
  if (!key) return { record: {}, isFirstRun: true };
  try {
    const raw = localStorage.getItem(key);
    if (raw) {
      const parsed = JSON.parse(raw);
      if (parsed && typeof parsed === 'object' && !Array.isArray(parsed)) {
        return { record: /** @type {Record<string, number>} */ (parsed), isFirstRun: false };
      }
    }
  } catch (error) {
    // Corrupt JSON or a thrown getItem (private mode, quota): a first run.
    console.warn('Announced awards: could not read the record:', error);
  }
  return { record: {}, isFirstRun: true };
}

/**
 * Is the BADGE family still in its first run — i.e. is its one retroactive
 * summary still owed?
 *
 * Deliberately NOT the same as `isFirstRun`. See `BADGE_RETRO_KEY`: the Home
 * catch-up seals the stat families' first run without sealing the badges', and
 * conflating the two would trade the summary for a pop-up storm.
 *
 * @param {Record<string, number>|null} [record]
 * @param {boolean} [isFirstRun]
 * @returns {boolean}
 */
export function badgesAreFirstRun(record, isFirstRun = false) {
  if (isFirstRun) return true;
  return !(record && record[BADGE_RETRO_KEY]);
}

/**
 * Are the five STAT families still in their first run — i.e. is the historical
 * backlog still owed its one summary screen?
 *
 * Deliberately NOT `isFirstRun`. See `STATS_SEEDED_KEY`: holding the legacy
 * badge key makes `isFirstRun` false while saying nothing whatever about whether
 * the stat families have been recorded.
 *
 * @param {Record<string, number>|null} [record]
 * @param {boolean} [isFirstRun]
 * @returns {boolean}
 */
export function statsAreFirstRun(record, isFirstRun = false) {
  if (isFirstRun) return true;
  return !(record && record[STATS_SEEDED_KEY]);
}

/**
 * Mark the stat families as seeded. Set by the Home catch-up's seal, and only
 * there — it is the one place that has actually looked at every family.
 * @param {Record<string, number>|null} [record]
 * @returns {Record<string, number>}
 */
export function markStatsSeeded(record) {
  return { ...(record || {}), [STATS_SEEDED_KEY]: 1 };
}

/**
 * Mark the retroactive badge summary as done. Called by the surface that
 * actually shows it (the stats page), never by a surface that only shows the
 * live pops.
 * @param {Record<string, number>|null} [record]
 * @returns {Record<string, number>}
 */
export function markBadgeRetroShown(record) {
  return { ...(record || {}), [BADGE_RETRO_KEY]: 1 };
}

/**
 * Persist the record. Best-effort: a failed write only means a moment may replay
 * next session, which beats throwing on a screen the player has just won.
 * No user id writes NOTHING — never a shared key.
 * @param {Record<string, number>} record
 * @param {string|null|undefined} userId whose record
 * @returns {boolean} whether it landed
 */
export function writeAnnouncedAwards(record, userId) {
  const key = announcedAwardsKey(userId);
  if (!key) return false;
  try {
    localStorage.setItem(key, JSON.stringify(record || {}));
    return true;
  } catch (error) {
    console.warn('Announced awards: could not save the record:', error);
    return false;
  }
}

/**
 * Record a drained queue. THE one write path.
 *
 * Re-reads immediately before merging rather than closing over a record captured
 * when the queue was planned. Two surfaces can drain a queue in one session (a
 * battle finishing, then a solve) and a stale capture would clobber whichever
 * wrote first.
 *
 * @param {Array|null|undefined} awards everything the player was actually shown
 * @param {string|null|undefined} userId whose record — none means nothing is written
 * @returns {Record<string, number>|null} the new record, or null if nothing was recorded
 */
export function recordAnnouncedAwards(awards, userId) {
  if (!Array.isArray(awards) || awards.length === 0) return null;
  if (!announcedAwardsKey(userId)) return null;
  const { record } = readAnnouncedAwards(userId);
  const next = mergeAnnouncedAwards(record, awards);
  writeAnnouncedAwards(next, userId);
  // The day these badges popped, for "Just earned" (badgeEarnedStamps). Only
  // DRAINED queues reach here — a first-run seal writes the record directly and
  // is a backlog, not news, so it deliberately stamps nothing.
  stampEarnedBadges(awards, userId);
  return next;
}
