// badgeScaling — the three ways a badge levels up, as factories.
//
// Extracted from badgeRegistry.js when the Easy On-Ramp block moved out to
// ./onRampBadges (Round 2 § D7): both files declare badges, so both need this
// vocabulary, and duplicating three factories is exactly how two halves of one
// catalog drift apart (one forgets `direction: 'ascending'` and its ladder
// silently inverts). Runtime-dependency-free on purpose — this module imports
// nothing, so any future block can be lifted out of the registry without
// creating a cycle.
//
// The TYPE lives here too, for the same reason: it is the shape these factories
// produce, and badgeRegistry re-declares it as an alias so every consumer that
// already imports `BadgeScaling` from the registry keeps working.

/**
 * How a badge levels up.
 *   `oneTime`        — earn it once; no tiers.
 *   `countLadder`    — repeat the feat N times (the existing `×N` flourish).
 *   `stageLadder`    — climb a list of named REQUIREMENTS (see below).
 *   `thresholdLadder`— reach a higher VALUE (streak length, margin, magnitude…).
 * `direction: 'descending'` marks ladders where LOWER is better (solve times).
 *
 * @typedef {Object} BadgeScaling
 * @property {'oneTime'|'countLadder'|'thresholdLadder'|'stageLadder'} type
 * @property {number[]} [tiers]
 * @property {'ascending'|'descending'} [direction]
 * @property {string[]} [tierNames]
 * @property {Record<number, string>} [tierIcons] Per-RUNG art override, keyed by
 *   0-based tier index, for the one ladder whose rungs are named identities
 *   rather than plain thresholds (the Speed ladder — § D2). Sparse on purpose:
 *   a rung with no entry wears the badge's own `icon`.
 * @property {{key: string, label: string, criteria?: string, needsPowerDice?: boolean, perfect?: boolean}[]} [stages] The
 *   ordered rung spec of a `stageLadder` — see the factory below. Absent on
 *   every other model. `criteria` is the plain-English phrase describing what a
 *   player actually DOES to reach that rung, with no trailing period and no
 *   mode ("Solve using at least one set of parentheses"); `tierCriteriaText`
 *   appends the badge's `criteriaScope`. It exists because the per-rung copy it
 *   replaced restated the rung's own label — see craftFeats.js.
 * @property {string} [unit]
 * @property {Record<number, number[]>} [perDuration] Battle-length-scaled tiers,
 *   keyed by `time_limit_seconds`. When present, `tiers` is the baseline row.
 * @property {number} [baseDurationSeconds]
 */

/** @returns {BadgeScaling} */
export const oneTime = () => ({ type: 'oneTime' });

/**
 * @param {number[]} tiers
 * @param {Partial<BadgeScaling>} [extra]
 * @returns {BadgeScaling}
 */
export const countLadder = (tiers, extra = {}) => ({ type: 'countLadder', tiers, ...extra });

/**
 * `direction` defaults to ascending HERE rather than at every call site, so a
 * threshold badge can never ship with an undeclared direction and be judged
 * backwards by `badgeTierIndex`.
 *
 * @param {number[]} tiers
 * @param {Partial<BadgeScaling>} [extra]
 * @returns {BadgeScaling}
 */
export const thresholdLadder = (tiers, extra = {}) =>
  ({ type: 'thresholdLadder', tiers, direction: 'ascending', ...extra });

/**
 * `stageLadder` — the rungs are REQUIREMENTS, not magnitudes of one measure.
 *
 * Added 2026-09-03 for the craft-family merge (design-per-mode-2026-08-31 § 2).
 * The other two models both judge ONE number: how many times you did the feat,
 * or how big the feat got. A merged craft family cannot: Full Spectrum climbs
 * "used an exponent" → "used all four operations" → "…ten times" → "all four
 * plus an exponent" → "…on a perfect solve". Those rungs are different
 * questions, so no single number orders them.
 *
 * The representation is deliberately boring: `tiers` is the ORDINAL list
 * `[1..n]`, so `badgeTierIndex(badge, highestRungReached)` — and every other
 * registry helper, and every renderer — keeps working with no change at all. The
 * derivation's job becomes "which is the highest rung this player has reached",
 * and it hands back that ordinal as the badge's value.
 *
 * `stages` is the ordered rung spec, low → high. Each stage carries:
 *   `key`   — the DETECTION key. The predicate for it lives in the derivation
 *             (src/stats-aggregation/craftStages.js), never here: this module
 *             stays pure metadata, exactly as the registry docblock requires. A
 *             test pins that the two sides declare the same key set, so a rung
 *             can never ship without a predicate (or vice versa).
 *   `label` — the rung's display name, mirrored into `tierNames` so `tierLabel`
 *             in components/stats/badgeVisuals.js renders it with no special
 *             case. Mirrored rather than read from `stages` for exactly that
 *             reason — the visual layer already knows `tierNames`.
 *   `criteria` — the plain-English act that reaches the rung, with no trailing
 *             period and no mode ("Solve using at least one set of
 *             parentheses"). `tierCriteriaText` appends the badge's
 *             `criteriaScope`. Optional so a stage ladder without per-rung copy
 *             still builds, but every craft rung carries one: the copy this
 *             replaced restated the rung's own LABEL, which is why 88 of the
 *             catalog's 198 rungs explained nothing (see craftFeats.js).
 *
 * @param {{key: string, label: string, criteria?: string, needsPowerDice?: boolean, perfect?: boolean}[]} stages
 * @param {Partial<BadgeScaling>} [extra]
 * @returns {BadgeScaling}
 */
export const stageLadder = (stages, extra = {}) => ({
  type: 'stageLadder',
  tiers: stages.map((_, index) => index + 1),
  direction: 'ascending',
  tierNames: stages.map((stage) => stage.label),
  stages: stages.map((stage) => ({ key: stage.key, label: stage.label, criteria: stage.criteria })),
  ...extra,
});
