// Badge IDENTITY colour — the third and innermost colour axis of the badge
// system (Round 2 § D1 / ask #1, David 2026-08-01).
//
//   "I would like to devise a unique color palette for each individual badge —
//    and a grouped theme outline around them … The important thing is that
//    badges all have a unique feel and very professional looking style."
//
// THREE AXES, THREE SURFACES, NO MUD. Each axis owns a different ring of the
// card, so they read simultaneously instead of competing:
//
//   MODE     → the card's border, background ramp and glow (the "grouped theme
//              outline"). Daily violet · Challenge cyan · Easy green ·
//              Solo Blitz blue · Blitz Battle magenta — the GAME's own mode
//              colours (see modeAccentVar in components/stats/badgeVisuals.js).
//   IDENTITY → the medal INSIDE that outline: the frame's fill, its stroke, its
//              outer bloom and the icon tint. This module.
//   RARITY   → the radial glow directly behind the art plus the tier caption
//              (`rarityVar` in ./badgeRarity). Unchanged.
//
// So: the CARD says which mode, the MEDAL says which badge, the GLOW says how
// rare. A player never has to disambiguate one from another because they never
// paint the same pixel.
//
// WHY DERIVED, NOT 56 HAND-PICKED HEXES. Hand-picking does not survive contact
// with a catalog that is still growing (R2-E adds Easy badges, R2-F adds Solo
// Blitz ones). A derived scheme means a new badge gets a coherent colour for
// free, and the set stays evenly spread instead of accumulating near-duplicates.
//
// THE SCHEME. Every badge sits at a point in its mode family's colour
// neighbourhood, described by two numbers:
//
//   HUE   — the mode's own hue, offset along a narrow ARC. The arcs are sized
//           from the real gaps between the five mode hues so that NO arc ever
//           reaches a neighbouring mode's hue: a Daily badge can never come out
//           looking like an Easy badge. Badges spread evenly across their arc in
//           registry order, so a section reads as one deliberate spectrum rather
//           than confetti.
//   BAND  — a (saturation, lightness) pair, cycling every three badges. The arcs
//           are necessarily narrow (the five mode hues are only 31–58° apart),
//           so hue alone cannot separate the 22 badges that share the Daily
//           family. The band is what actually makes neighbours distinguishable;
//           hue is what keeps the family coherent.
//
// HONEST LIMIT, STATED: with 22 badges inside a 24°-wide arc, identity colour is
// NOT a lookup key — you cannot name a badge from its colour alone, and it is
// not meant to be. The ICON is the identifier (one curated drawing per badge,
// 100% coverage). Identity colour is what stops a wall of same-coloured tiles
// from reading as one undifferentiated block.

/**
 * Hue anchors, in degrees, for each badge family.
 *
 * These are the GAME's mode colours from ui/theme/tokens.css — NOT the
 * badge-screen overrides Slice 4 added, which Round 2 reverts. They are stored
 * as numbers because the scheme does hue ARITHMETIC on them, which a
 * `var(--u37-*)` string cannot express. `badgeIdentity.test.js` parses
 * tokens.css, converts each mode hex to HSL and fails if any anchor drifts more
 * than a degree from its token — so tokens.css remains the single source of
 * truth even though the number is repeated here.
 *
 * `arc` is the HALF-width in degrees. Each is bounded by the distance to the
 * nearest neighbouring mode hue, minus a guard, so the five arcs are disjoint:
 *   green 153.1 ─31°─ cyan 184.0 ─34°─ blue 218.1 ─58°─ violet 276.1
 *   (cyan is CHALLENGE and violet is the DAILY ARENA since 2026-08-31; the two
 *    anchors were swapped, so the spacing above is unchanged.)
 *   ─37°─ magenta 313.3 ─(200°, the wrap)─ green
 */
// Each `arc` is pushed as wide as CONTAINMENT allows and no wider: two arcs may
// never overlap, and no badge may end up nearer a foreign mode's hue than its
// own. Both are pinned by tests, which is what stops a future "make them more
// colourful" edit from quietly letting Daily badges drift into Easy's green.
const FAMILY_HUE = {
  easy: { hue: 153.1, arc: 14, token: '--u37-green' },
  challenge: { hue: 184.0, arc: 14, token: '--u37-cyan' },
  soloBlitz: { hue: 218.1, arc: 16, token: '--u37-blue' },
  daily: { hue: 276.1, arc: 18, token: '--u37-violet' },
  blitzBattle: { hue: 313.3, arc: 18, token: '--u37-magenta' },
};

/**
 * Registry mode → identity FAMILY. Only `crossArena` is not its own family: it
 * is the Colosseum capstone and rides the Blitz Battle chip in the showcase, so
 * it must draw from the Colosseum's arc AND be ranked among its badges — sharing
 * an arc without sharing the ranking would let it land on top of another badge.
 * `badgeIdentity.test.js` pins this against `SHOWCASE_MODES` so the two groupings
 * can never disagree.
 */
const MODE_FAMILY = {
  daily: 'daily',
  challenge: 'challenge',
  easy: 'easy',
  soloBlitz: 'soloBlitz',
  blitzBattle: 'blitzBattle',
  crossArena: 'blitzBattle',
};

/**
 * The (saturation, lightness) bands, cycling by rank.
 *
 * Two constraints fixed these numbers, and both are pinned by tests:
 *   · CONTRAST — the darkest band must still clear 3:1 against the cosmic
 *     background (#05030f) at every hue, the WCAG 1.4.11 bar for a graphical
 *     object. The binding case is deep blue (Solo Blitz's arc), which lands at
 *     3.24:1; anything darker fails there first.
 *   · NOT-LOCKED — the lightness spread is deliberately only 17 points. A locked
 *     card renders its accent at ~8% against near-black, so even the deepest
 *     band stays unmistakably "lit". A wider spread would make an earned band-2
 *     badge read as unearned, which is a far worse bug than a duller colour.
 */
const BANDS = [
  { sat: 90, light: 74 },
  { sat: 76, light: 65 },
  { sat: 94, light: 57 },
];

/** The default for a mode no family claims — Challenge's identity family. */
const FALLBACK_FAMILY = 'challenge';

/**
 * @typedef {Object} BadgeIdentity
 * @property {number} hue   Degrees, 0–360.
 * @property {number} sat   Percent.
 * @property {number} light Percent.
 * @property {string} css   The ready-to-paint `hsl(H S% L%)` string.
 * @property {string} family Which mode family's neighbourhood it sits in.
 */

/**
 * The identity family a badge draws from.
 * @param {{mode?: string}} [badge]
 * @returns {string}
 */
export function badgeFamily(badge) {
  return MODE_FAMILY[badge?.mode] ?? FALLBACK_FAMILY;
}

/**
 * Build one identity from a family and a position within it.
 *
 * `rank` is 0-based and `total` is the family's size, so the badges spread
 * evenly from one end of the arc to the other. A family of one sits dead on its
 * mode's hue rather than at an arbitrary edge.
 *
 * @param {string} family
 * @param {number} rank
 * @param {number} total
 * @returns {BadgeIdentity}
 */
export function identityFor(family, rank, total) {
  const anchor = FAMILY_HUE[family] ?? FAMILY_HUE[FALLBACK_FAMILY];
  const safeTotal = Math.max(1, total);
  const safeRank = Math.min(Math.max(rank, 0), safeTotal - 1);

  // t walks -1 → +1 across the arc; a one-badge family sits at the centre.
  const t = safeTotal === 1 ? 0 : (safeRank / (safeTotal - 1)) * 2 - 1;
  const hue = (anchor.hue + anchor.arc * t + 360) % 360;

  const band = BANDS[safeRank % BANDS.length];
  const rounded = Math.round(hue * 10) / 10;
  return {
    hue: rounded,
    sat: band.sat,
    light: band.light,
    css: `hsl(${rounded} ${band.sat}% ${band.light}%)`,
    family,
  };
}

/**
 * Attach an `identity` to every badge in a catalog.
 *
 * Called ONCE, by the registry, over the whole catalog — so `identity` is a real
 * per-badge field on every entry (`badge.identity.css` is what the components
 * paint with) rather than something each consumer has to remember to derive.
 * Ranking is by REGISTRY ORDER within the family, which is also grid order, so
 * a section paints as a smooth walk along its arc.
 *
 * Adding a badge re-spreads its family across the same arc — every sibling
 * shifts by a degree or two and the set stays evenly distributed. That is the
 * intended behaviour: no lane that adds badges has to re-tune anything.
 *
 * @template {{mode?: string}} T
 * @param {T[]} badges
 * @returns {(T & {identity: BadgeIdentity})[]}
 */
export function assignBadgeIdentities(badges) {
  /** @type {Record<string, number>} */
  const totals = {};
  for (const badge of badges) {
    const family = badgeFamily(badge);
    totals[family] = (totals[family] ?? 0) + 1;
  }

  /** @type {Record<string, number>} */
  const seen = {};
  return badges.map((badge) => {
    const family = badgeFamily(badge);
    const rank = seen[family] ?? 0;
    seen[family] = rank + 1;
    return { ...badge, identity: identityFor(family, rank, totals[family]) };
  });
}

/**
 * One badge's paint-ready identity colour, safe on a missing/undecorated badge.
 * Every registry badge carries `identity` already; this is the guard for the
 * component boundary, where `badge` can legitimately be null mid-render.
 * @param {{identity?: BadgeIdentity, mode?: string}} [badge]
 * @returns {string}
 */
export function badgeIdentityColor(badge) {
  if (badge?.identity?.css) return badge.identity.css;
  // No identity (a hand-built test double, or a null badge): fall back to the
  // family's own hue at the middle band rather than to the mode accent — the
  // frame would otherwise silently collapse back onto the outline colour, which
  // is exactly the flatness this axis exists to remove.
  return identityFor(badgeFamily(badge), 0, 1).css;
}

export { FAMILY_HUE, MODE_FAMILY, BANDS };
