// solveFailure — make a failed solve-path write VISIBLE.
//
// WHY THIS EXISTS. `captureError` (src/observability) had ZERO production callers
// across the whole codebase, and Sentry runs with `tracesSampleRate: 0`. Every
// hand-written `catch` in `src/` therefore reported to `console.*` and nothing
// else — invisible on a player's phone, invisible in Sentry.
//
// Three of those swallows sit on the path a player takes when they WIN, which is
// the worst possible place for a silent failure:
//
//   * the anti-spoof leaderboard write — fails, and the player sees a normal win
//     with rocket and celebration while their time is simply not on the board;
//   * the post-solve stats save — fails, `setResultsReady(true)` runs anyway so
//     the celebration proceeds, and the solve is silently unsaved;
//   * the day's dice read — failed, and the player WAS handed locally-rolled
//     dice matching no other player's board, with nothing on screen saying so.
//     ⚠️ That stopped being true on 2026-09-16: the read failure is now recorded
//     and start()/resumeRecovered() REFUSE, so no board is ever shown. The stage
//     is still reported to Sentry, but it no longer speaks to the player — see
//     PLAYER_MESSAGE below for why that is the whole point.
//
// This was not theoretical. On 2026-09-02 David solved Expert on staging, the
// client wrongly accepted a 2-of-3 solve, the server correctly rejected it, and
// THIS swallow is why he saw a full win with no leaderboard row and no stats. The
// rule bug is fixed (2f6f918); the silence was the other half.
//
// TWO AUDIENCES, ONE CALL.
//   1. Sentry, always — so a failure that a player shrugs off is still countable.
//   2. The player, but ONLY when their RESULT is at stake (David's ruling): a
//      lost rank or a lost solve is worth interrupting for; a stale avatar is
//      not. `playerFacing: false` stages report to Sentry and stay quiet.
//
// A toast rather than an `alert()`: the Toaster is mounted app-wide
// (routing/App.jsx) and a toast does not hijack the win screen the way a modal
// alert would.
//
// This module must NEVER throw. It is called from inside catch blocks, and an
// error reporter that can itself fail turns a lost leaderboard row into a broken
// win screen.

import { captureError } from './sentry';
import { toast } from '@/ui/primitives/use-toast';
// ⚠️ THE LEAF, NOT THE BARREL. `@/daily-challenge` would drag the entire module
// — DailyChallengeGame and all — into every consumer of @/observability, because
// `useDailyChallengeAttempt` imports this file. That is not theoretical: it broke
// 61 passing tests in useDailyChallengeAttempt.test.jsx the moment it was written
// as a barrel import, with an error about an unrelated `@/dice` mock. madge finds
// no cycle, so the only symptom is a graph that quietly triples in size.
//
// Deep-importing a leaf constant module is existing practice here (staleDailyNotice
// reaches for @/game-modes/modeKeys and @/leaderboard/dailyModeTabs the same way).
import { DAILY_LIMIT_LABEL } from '@/daily-challenge/dailyLimit';

/** The solve-path failures worth reporting. */
export const SOLVE_STAGE = Object.freeze({
  /** submit-daily-time rejected or errored — no leaderboard row for this solve. */
  leaderboardWrite: 'leaderboard-write',
  /** The post-solve game_stats save failed — the solve itself is unrecorded. */
  statsSave: 'stats-save',
  /** The day's dice could not be read — the board shown matches nobody else's. */
  diceRead: 'dice-read',
});

/**
 * What the PLAYER is told, per stage. A stage absent from this map is reported
 * to Sentry and never surfaced — that is the "cosmetic failure" case.
 *
 * `leaderboardWrite` is deliberately ABSENT here: one sentence cannot be true of
 * every way that write fails. It is resolved by code below.
 */
const PLAYER_MESSAGE = Object.freeze({
  [SOLVE_STAGE.statsSave]: {
    title: 'We could not save that solve',
    description: 'Your stats and streak may not include it. Check your connection and try again.',
  },
});

// ⚠️ `diceRead` IS DELIBERATELY ABSENT from the map above — Sentry only.
//
// It used to say:
//   "Today’s puzzle could not be loaded"
//   "These dice are not today’s official board, so this solve will not be ranked."
//
// Both sentences described the OLD behaviour, where a failed read handed the
// player locally-rolled dice and let them play on. They have been false since
// the 2026-09-16 fix: `useDailyChallengeAttempt` records the failure and both
// start() and resumeRecovered() refuse on it. So there is no board, nothing was
// solved, and there is no solve to rank. David, seeing it on a phone on
// 2026-09-17: "it is saying the wrong thing… we shouldn’t show a puzzle if the
// daily can’t load for some reason, not a wrong puzzle."
//
// It is REMOVED rather than reworded, because a second message here would be
// wrong at any wording. All three playable paths already alert
// DICE_UNAVAILABLE_MESSAGE ("Couldn’t load today’s puzzle — check your
// connection and try again. Your attempt has not been used.") and refuse.
// Telling the player twice about one failure, from two layers, in two different
// voices, is exactly what he saw on screen.
//
// The CALLER owns this message because only the caller knows whether the player
// was about to play. `fetchTodaysDice` also serves the stats-recap path and
// handleNewGame, where the player's result is not at stake at all — and the rule
// at the top of this file is that we interrupt only when it is.

// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// THE LEADERBOARD MESSAGE, BY WHAT ACTUALLY HAPPENED
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// This used to be one line for every outcome:
//
//   "Your solve counted, but it could not be added to today's board.
//    Check your connection."
//
// For an `expired` refusal BOTH halves are false. Nothing counted — the hour had
// closed and the server refused the solve outright — and the connection was
// fine; the request arrived and was answered. Telling someone to check their
// connection sends them to restart their router over a rule they were never
// told about, and the sentence before it tells them they won something they did
// not. That is worse than saying nothing.
//
// `submit-daily-time` answers domain refusals IN-BAND (HTTP 200 with a code), so
// the code is always available at the call site and always says which of these
// happened. The three groups below are genuinely different events and the player
// can act on exactly one of them:
//
//   • A RULE refused it   — nothing counted, and why is worth knowing.
//   • OUR SIDE broke      — nothing the player can do; do not send them to
//                           their router for our 500.
//   • WE COULD NOT ASK    — the only case where "check your connection" is
//                           honest, and even then it is "may not", not "did not":
//                           a throw does not prove the row is missing, which is
//                           why leaderboardSubmit checks the table before
//                           alarming at all.
const LEADERBOARD_MESSAGE_BY_CODE = Object.freeze({
  expired: {
    // ⚠️ THE NUMBER COMES FROM daily-challenge/dailyLimit, NOT FROM HERE.
    // This copy said "your hour" while the clock on screen said 15:37 would be a
    // player told two different rules by the same app about the same solve — and
    // this message is shown at the exact moment the rule costs them the day, so
    // it is the worst possible place to be stale.
    //
    // ⚠️ IT CAN STILL SAY SOMETHING THE PLAYER DID NOT EXPERIENCE, and that is
    // NOT a bug in this file. `expired` is the SERVER's verdict, and the server
    // window is deliberately still 3600s until the production OTA carrying 15:37
    // reaches players (see dailyLimit.js). So a player on an old build who takes
    // 40 minutes is refused by a server saying "over an hour" while reading copy
    // that says 15:37. That window closes when the server is tightened — tracked
    // at .workshop/FOLLOWUP-daily-window-server.md.
    title: 'Your time ran out',
    description: `Today’s Daily closes ${DAILY_LIMIT_LABEL} after you start it, and the clock keeps running even when the app is closed — so this solve was not counted.`,
  },
  already_forfeited: {
    title: 'Today’s Daily had already ended',
    description: 'This attempt was forfeited earlier, so the solve could not be added to today’s board.',
  },
  no_attempt: {
    title: 'We have no record of today’s attempt',
    description: 'The server never saw this Daily start, so it could not accept the solve. Starting the Daily again will not help — today’s attempt is spent.',
  },
  too_fast: {
    title: 'That solve was too fast to record',
    description: 'A Daily needs at least two seconds between starting and submitting.',
  },
  invalid_solve: {
    title: 'That solve was not accepted',
    description: 'The server could not verify this equation against today’s dice.',
  },
});

/** Codes that mean OUR side failed, not the player's and not a rule. */
const SERVER_FAULT_CODES = new Set([
  'no_challenge', 'write_failed', 'internal_error', 'config_error', 'unauthorized',
]);

const LEADERBOARD_SERVER_FAULT = Object.freeze({
  title: 'We could not save your time',
  description: 'Something went wrong on our side, so this solve is not on today’s board. Nothing you did caused it.',
});

const LEADERBOARD_REJECTED = Object.freeze({
  title: 'Your time was not added to the leaderboard',
  description: 'The server did not accept this solve for today’s board.',
});

const LEADERBOARD_UNREACHABLE = Object.freeze({
  title: 'Your time may not have reached the leaderboard',
  description: 'We could not confirm it was added to today’s board. Check your connection.',
});

/**
 * The right sentence for this failure.
 *
 * @param {string} stage
 * @param {Record<string, unknown>} context
 * @returns {{title: string, description: string}|undefined}
 */
function playerMessage(stage, context) {
  if (stage !== SOLVE_STAGE.leaderboardWrite) return PLAYER_MESSAGE[stage];

  const code = typeof context.code === 'string' ? context.code : null;
  if (code && LEADERBOARD_MESSAGE_BY_CODE[code]) return LEADERBOARD_MESSAGE_BY_CODE[code];
  if (code && SERVER_FAULT_CODES.has(code)) return LEADERBOARD_SERVER_FAULT;

  // No code at all means the port threw — we never got an answer. An unknown
  // code means the server refused for a reason this build has not heard of, and
  // inventing a cause for it would be the original bug in a new costume.
  return context.rejected ? LEADERBOARD_REJECTED : LEADERBOARD_UNREACHABLE;
}

/** Coerce anything a catch block might hand us into a real Error for Sentry. */
function asError(value, stage) {
  if (value instanceof Error) return value;
  try {
    return new Error(`${stage}: ${typeof value === 'string' ? value : JSON.stringify(value)}`);
  } catch {
    return new Error(`${stage}: unserialisable failure`);
  }
}

/**
 * Report a solve-path failure to Sentry, and tell the player when their result
 * is at stake.
 *
 * ⚠️ `context.alarmed === false` REPORTS BUT DOES NOT TELL THE PLAYER. It is for
 * the case where the caller has established that the player's result is NOT at
 * stake despite the failure — the leaderboard row is already there, written by
 * this call or by the fallback writer beside it. Chandler (2026-09-06) watched a
 * win screen behind a red "your time was not saved" banner for a solve that WAS
 * saved; see game-flow/leaderboardSubmit for how that happened.
 *
 * The suppression is deliberately one-directional: it silences the TOAST and
 * never the Sentry report. An unexplained rejection is still a thing we need to
 * see, and the obvious fix — stop toasting on rejections — would have buried
 * this bug instead of surfacing it.
 *
 * @param {string} stage one of SOLVE_STAGE
 * @param {unknown} error whatever the catch block caught
 * @param {Record<string, unknown>} [context] extra Sentry context (mode, date…);
 *   `alarmed: false` suppresses the player-facing toast only. For
 *   `leaderboardWrite`, `code` selects WHICH sentence the player sees and
 *   `rejected` distinguishes "the server refused" from "we never got an answer" —
 *   omit them and the player gets the honest cannot-confirm wording.
 * @returns {boolean} whether the player was shown anything
 */
export function reportSolveFailure(stage, error, context = {}) {
  let shown = false;
  try {
    captureError(asError(error, stage), { stage, ...context });
  } catch {
    // Reporting must never be the thing that breaks a win screen.
  }
  try {
    const message = playerMessage(stage, context);
    if (message && context.alarmed !== false) {
      toast({ ...message, variant: 'destructive' });
      shown = true;
    }
  } catch {
    // As above — a failed toast must not escape into the solve path.
  }
  return shown;
}
