// The Daily board's MODE axis — the pills that pick which of the three Dailies
// a board is showing.
//
// WHY THIS IS ITS OWN MODULE and not a literal inside DailyTab: four boards
// (Global, Friends, Equations Used, National Average) and the podiums all split
// by mode, and the sub-tab list, the empty states and the tests all need the
// same labels. One copy, derived from the frozen key list, so adding a fourth
// Daily is a change in `@/game-modes` and nowhere here.
//
// ⚠️ The keys come from DAILY_MODE_KEYS, which is PLAY order (easiest first) —
// not alphabetical and not "legacy first". Reordering the pills means reordering
// that array, deliberately, in one place.
//
// The labels are the DIFFICULTY tier, not the wire key: a player picking a board
// is choosing Easy / Challenge / Expert, and the fact that Challenge's wire key
// is the bare string 'daily' is a storage detail they must never see. `modeOf`
// is the one place that translation lives.

import { DAILY_MODE_KEYS, MODE_TIER_LABELS, modeOf } from '@/game-modes';
import { dailyModeAccent } from './modeAccent';

/**
 * Challenge's Daily — the legacy wire key, and the board every component here
 * defaults to.
 *
 * A deliberate MIRROR of the constant in src/stats-aggregation/leaderboard.js,
 * not an import of it. A component reaching into the fetch module for a string
 * drags the whole read path (transport, player, the edge-call port) into
 * every board's import graph for one word. The two are pinned equal by a test,
 * the same way DAILY_MODE_KEYS is pinned to the Deno side's DAILY_MODES.
 */
export const DEFAULT_DAILY_MODE = 'daily';

/**
 * Difficulty tier → the word a player sees.
 *
 * A RE-EXPORT now, not a definition: the labels moved to `@/game-modes` beside
 * the frozen key list when the podium celebration needed them outside the
 * leaderboard, and importing this barrel for three words would have dragged
 * every board component into game-flow's import graph. One definition beats a
 * mirror-and-pin pair.
 */
export const DAILY_MODE_LABELS = MODE_TIER_LABELS;

/**
 * The player-facing name of a daily wire key.
 *
 * `modeOf` coerces anything unrecognised to 'easy', which is the safe direction
 * for RENDERING (a label, not a write gate) — a board never crashes over a stale
 * mode string, it just shows the gentlest one.
 *
 * @param {string} key a daily wire key ('daily_easy' | 'daily' | 'daily_expert')
 * @returns {string}
 */
export const dailyModeLabel = (key) => DAILY_MODE_LABELS[modeOf(key)];

/**
 * SubTabBar options for the three Dailies, in play order — each wearing its own
 * TIER colour: Easy green, Challenge cyan, Expert gold.
 *
 * ⚠️ THIS REVERSES WHAT THIS MODULE USED TO ARGUE, and the old argument is worth
 * keeping because it was right when it was made. It said: one shared accent, on
 * purpose, because the sub-tab row below already carries a colour per BOARD
 * (Global cyan, Friends green, Equations magenta, National violet) so the active
 * pill previews the board you are opening — and stacking a second colour
 * language on top of that would leave neither readable. The mode row therefore
 * wore the Daily's own cyan and distinguished itself by position and by label.
 *
 * What broke it: Challenge MOVED ONTO cyan (David, 2026-08-31). The one shared
 * accent WAS cyan, so from that day an Expert board was labelled in Challenge's
 * colour — measured, not inferred: the selected pill rendered rgb(31,240,255) on
 * all three modes. The row had stopped meaning "the Daily" and started meaning
 * "Challenge", on every board.
 *
 * David's resolution: "tier colours win; the per-board colour language gives
 * way." So there is now ONE colour language on this surface and it is the tier.
 * The boards keep their identity in their labels and icons (Globe · Users ·
 * Percent · Flag), which they always had; the tiers had nothing else.
 *
 * All three pills show their own colour AT ONCE rather than the row taking the
 * selected tier's — SubTabBar paints every pill in its accent and separates
 * active from inactive by intensity, so a player sees green/cyan/gold side by
 * side and learns the mapping without selecting anything.
 */
export const DAILY_MODE_TABS = Object.freeze(
  DAILY_MODE_KEYS.map((id) => Object.freeze({
    id,
    label: dailyModeLabel(id),
    accent: dailyModeAccent(id),
  })),
);
