// transport/withTimeout — bound a network promise that gates the UI.
//
// WHY THIS EXISTS
// ───────────────
// Two awaits sat on the boot path with no time limit at all, and each one
// could hold the entire app on a spinner forever:
//
//   • AuthContext's getSession() → supabase.auth.signInAnonymously()
//     `isLoadingAuth` is cleared in that function's `finally`, so a request
//     that never settles means it is never cleared.
//   • App.jsx's fetchUser() → apiClient.auth.me() → supabase.auth.getUser()
//     `loadingUser` is likewise cleared in a `finally`.
//
// App.jsx renders <FullScreenLoader /> while either flag is true. A hung
// request therefore produces an endless spinner with no controls and no way
// out — which is what a reviewer reports as "an unresponsive screen".
//
// A rejection is NOT the failure mode being defended against here: a rejected
// promise still runs the `finally`, so the app recovers. The dangerous case is
// a promise that simply never settles — a connection that is accepted and then
// left open, which is exactly what a captive portal, a VPN, or a filtering
// proxy does. Supabase's client sets no timeout of its own.
//
// FAIL OPEN, NEVER THROW. Timing out yields the caller's fallback value so the
// app carries on as an unauthenticated visitor, showing a UI the user can
// actually touch. Being wrongly treated as signed-out is recoverable — the
// welcome screen has a Log In button. A frozen spinner is not.
//
// See:
//   docs/bugs.md  (2026-09-23 — iPad launch screen entry)

// Generous on purpose: long enough that a genuinely slow connection still
// completes normally and nobody is wrongly signed out, short enough that a
// hung request cannot pass for a broken app. Real auth calls finish in well
// under a second.
export const BOOT_NETWORK_TIMEOUT_MS = 10000;

/**
 * Resolve `promise`, or `fallback` if it has not settled within `ms`.
 *
 * Never rejects for the timeout itself. A promise that REJECTS still rejects —
 * callers already handle that, and swallowing real errors here would hide them.
 *
 * @template T
 * @param {Promise<T>} promise
 * @param {T} fallback     value to resolve with if time runs out
 * @param {number} [ms]
 * @returns {Promise<T>}
 */
export function withTimeout(promise, fallback, ms = BOOT_NETWORK_TIMEOUT_MS) {
  let timer;
  const ticker = new Promise((resolve) => {
    timer = setTimeout(() => resolve(fallback), ms);
  });
  // `finally` runs on both settle paths, so the timer is always cleared and a
  // pending timeout can never keep the process (or a test runner) alive.
  return Promise.race([promise, ticker]).finally(() => clearTimeout(timer));
}
