// The ONE ordering rule for a day's board.
//
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// 🔴 THE BUG THIS EXISTS TO KILL
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// A day's board was ordered by `solve_time_seconds` alone in three places, and
// the podium was credited by a DIFFERENT rule in a fourth.
//
//   • The server pages the rows with `ORDER BY solve_time_seconds, user_id` —
//     `user_id` purely so pagination is stable and no row is skipped across
//     pages (it is unique per date).
//   • `getLeaderboard` then re-sorted client-side by TIME ONLY. JS sort is
//     stable, so tied rows kept the order they arrived in — i.e. **user_id**
//     order, which is a UUID and therefore arbitrary.
//   • But `rankDate` (supabase/functions/_shared/podiumFinishes.ts), which is
//     what actually credits a medal inside `get-player-stats`, breaks a tie on
//     the EARLIEST `created_at`. Its rule is the long-standing one: equal times,
//     first submission wins.
//
// Solve times are whole seconds, so ties are ordinary rather than exotic. On any
// tied board the displayed rank and the credited medal could disagree — the home
// launcher's podium banner (`getPodium`), the stats page's rank, the share
// card's "#N today", and `getUserPodiumFinish` all read the array order.
//
// Nobody noticed because a disagreement is invisible unless you hold both
// numbers at once — which is exactly what the podium CELEBRATION now does. It
// ranks with the podium rule, so without this fix it would have congratulated a
// player for a placement the banner beside it denied.
//
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// THE FIX
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// One comparator, defined here, used by every consumer: `getLeaderboard`'s sort
// and the podium ranker. The tie-break rule now exists once, so the display and
// the medal cannot drift apart again.
//
// It is deliberately a MIRROR of `rankDate`'s comparator rather than an import
// of it — that file is Deno and this is browser ESM, the same reason
// `modeKeys.js` mirrors `dailyCoverage.ts`. The two are pinned by
// game-flow/podiumCatchUp.drift.test.js, which is allowed to import the .ts.

/**
 * Compare two board rows in PODIUM order: fastest first, and on equal times the
 * earliest submission first.
 *
 * A row with a missing or unparseable `created_at` sorts last among its tied
 * group rather than winning by accident — the same direction `rankDate` takes,
 * and the safe one: a row we cannot date must not be handed a medal over one we
 * can.
 *
 * @param {{solve_time_seconds?: number|null, created_at?: string|null}} a
 * @param {{solve_time_seconds?: number|null, created_at?: string|null}} b
 * @returns {number}
 */
export function comparePodiumOrder(a, b) {
  const byTime = (a?.solve_time_seconds ?? 0) - (b?.solve_time_seconds ?? 0);
  if (byTime !== 0) return byTime;

  const at = stampOf(a);
  const bt = stampOf(b);
  // Equal stamps — including two UNDATEABLE rows, which are both +Infinity.
  //
  // ⚠️ This case is why `stampOf` exists. `rankDate` inlines the same idea as
  // `at - bt` after defaulting a missing date to +Infinity, and
  // `Infinity - Infinity` is **NaN**, not 0 — a comparator returning NaN has
  // implementation-defined sort behaviour. In practice V8 leaves the pair where
  // it found it, which is the same answer 0 gives, so the two agree and the
  // drift test proves it on an all-undateable board. Returning 0 outright is
  // simply the version that says what it means.
  if (at === bt) return 0;
  if (!Number.isFinite(at)) return 1;
  if (!Number.isFinite(bt)) return -1;
  return at - bt;
}

/**
 * A row's submission time in ms, or +Infinity when it has none we can use.
 *
 * ABSENT and UNPARSEABLE collapse to the same bucket on purpose: both mean "we
 * cannot date this row", and a row we cannot date must not take a medal from one
 * we can.
 */
function stampOf(row) {
  if (!row?.created_at) return Number.POSITIVE_INFINITY;
  const ms = Date.parse(row.created_at);
  return Number.isNaN(ms) ? Number.POSITIVE_INFINITY : ms;
}

/**
 * A board in podium order. Returns a NEW array — the callers below hand their
 * result straight to React, and sorting a fetched array in place would mutate a
 * cached read.
 *
 * @template {{solve_time_seconds?: number|null, created_at?: string|null}} T
 * @param {T[]} rows
 * @returns {T[]}
 */
export function sortByPodiumOrder(rows) {
  return [...(rows || [])].sort(comparePodiumOrder);
}
