// M3 — server-authoritative battle config + feature flag.
//
// The duration picker (CreateChallenge) and the `time_limit_seconds`
// create-payload field are gated OFF behind BATTLE_DURATIONS_ENABLED until:
//   1. Lane B's migration adds the `time_limit_seconds` + `battle_started_at`
//      columns to head_to_head_challenges. Writing an unknown column makes the
//      Supabase insert fail, so sending the field before the column exists
//      would break battle creation entirely.
//   2. David verifies a real two-player staging battle end-to-end (M3 is
//      security-critical; the RLS lockdown + two-player verify are David-gated).
//
// Flag OFF ⇒ battle creation behaves EXACTLY as before the M3 work: a 10-minute
// race, no new payload field.
// ENABLED 2026-07-27: both gates satisfied — (1) the columns are applied on
// staging AND prod (verified by direct probe), and (2) the battle timer is wired
// to honor `time_limit_seconds` (commit 13db047) and a two-player staging verify
// confirmed a 3-min pick stores 180, no-field defaults to 600, and the opponent
// receives the stored duration. Server-authoritative finalize remains a SEPARATE
// flag (BATTLE_SERVER_RESULT_ENABLED, still off) — independent of durations.
export const BATTLE_DURATIONS_ENABLED = true;

// M3 — route battle finalize through the server-authoritative
// `submit-battle-result` edge fn instead of the legacy client-side write in
// battleWrites.js. Gated OFF until:
//   1. The F4↔B contract is LOCKED and B's `submit-battle-result` fn is live
//      (see .workshop/m3-battle-result-contract-proposal-2026-07-26.md).
//   2. David verifies a real two-player staging battle end-to-end.
//   3. Only THEN does B land the phase-2 RLS lockdown of direct client writes.
// OFF ⇒ finalize uses the existing client-write path unchanged (merge-safe now).
export const BATTLE_SERVER_RESULT_ENABLED = false;

// Legacy default battle length (10 minutes), used when the picker is disabled.
export const DEFAULT_BATTLE_SECONDS = 600;

// Expert battles + solos. OFF until the vetted deck pool exists.
//
// ⚠️ BATTLES HAVE NO KILL-SWITCH. `HeadToHead` is a plain mounted route
// (routing/pages.config.js), so whatever lands in the mode picker is INSTANTLY
// live to every player on that build — there is no "ship it dark and enable it
// later" for battles. That is why Expert needs its own flag and this one is it.
//
// WHAT THIS FLAG ACTUALLY GATES (corrected 2026-09-10). It used to say "THE
// PICKER ONLY". No picker reads it. Its one production reader is
// `HeadToHeadStats.jsx:49`, which filters 'expert' out of the stats TAB list —
// so today the flag hides Expert's stats tabs, and the mode picker is gated
// elsewhere. Worth stating precisely, because "gates the picker" invites someone
// to flip it believing stats are unaffected.
//
// Every READING path already handles 'expert' correctly (blitzMode.js and the
// surfaces that ask it), and the Expert buckets are DERIVED regardless of this
// flag — it hides an already-computed number rather than preventing the
// computation. So a row written by a newer build never renders wrong on an older
// one. That ordering is deliberate: reads first, writes second.
//
// ✅ TURNED ON 2026-09-11 (David: "i also still do not see anything within the
// blitz colosseum about expert mode battles ... please incorporate that into
// .302", and "this expert battle needs to be in the colosseum, on the blitz
// stats page, and within peoples public profile pages"). Against the three
// conditions below, measured rather than assumed:
//
//   1. ✅ VERIFIED on prod the same day: `select count(*) from
//      public.expert_deck_pool` returned 5000. A deck is 250, so the pool is 20
//      decks deep and the banking cron is ahead of demand. "Vetted" here means
//      the sampler actually OBSERVED a perfect solve for that roll, which is the
//      specific guarantee that stops a race wedging on an unsolvable puzzle.
//   2. ✅ Unchanged and still true — see below.
//   3. ✅ DONE 2026-09-11, and it never needed two humans after all.
//      `expertBattle.integration.test.js` plays the whole race against real
//      staging with TWO REAL ACCOUNTS holding two independent sessions: A deals
//      a full vetted deck under its own JWT, creates the battle, B accepts, both
//      read the board back separately and are asserted to see identical dice,
//      both solve with genuine perfect solves found by the app's own engine,
//      and the shipping client finalize resolves the winner. It drives the path
//      that is ON (client finalize) rather than the one behind
//      BATTLE_SERVER_RESULT_ENABLED, which is what the older
//      submitBattleResult suite covers.
//
//      The condition was written as a manual step and stayed unmet for months
//      because of it. A battle is not simultaneous at the data layer — each
//      player writes their own result column — so two sessions answer it fully,
//      and unlike a manual run they answer it again on every CI-less
//      `npm run test:integration`.
//
//      That suite also carries the check this flag most needs: it runs the
//      SOLVER over the dice actually dealt and asserts a perfect solve exists
//      for each. Expert's deck index does not advance until someone solves the
//      current puzzle, so an unsolvable roll hangs the race permanently. The
//      pool claims that property; the test verifies it independently, with the
//      same engine that judges the player at runtime.
//
// ⚠️ FLIPPING THIS ALONE USED TO DO ALMOST NOTHING, and that is the trap this
// note exists to close. Every picker hardcoded its own two-item tier list, so
// the flag moved the stats tabs and the profile card and left the Colosseum
// unchanged — which is exactly what David reported seeing. Pickers now read
// `offeredBlitzTiers()` (blitzTierPresentation.js), which is the single place
// the gate is applied.
//
// TO TURN IT ON, all three must be true:
//   1. The vetted Expert deck pool exists and is banking ahead of demand.
//      Expert requires a PERFECT solve, and an unvetted roll may have no findable
//      one — in a race whose index does not advance until someone finds it.
//   2. `nextBattleDice` returns null past the deck for Expert (it does) AND the
//      caller ends the run (it does) — so exhaustion can never deal an unvetted
//      puzzle.
//   3. A two-player Expert battle has been played end-to-end on staging.
//
// ⚠️ NOT a precondition, and do not conflate them: BATTLE_SERVER_RESULT_ENABLED
// stays OFF. Expert ships on the same client finalize path all 71 production
// battles have used.
export const BATTLE_EXPERT_ENABLED = true;

// Length of the pre-generated `dice_sequence` stored on every battle. Both
// creation paths (CreateChallenge + ChallengeFriend) MUST use this so the two
// players read the SAME stored array for every realistically-reachable puzzle,
// and so the server-verify (`submitBattleResult`, which re-solves each solution
// against `dice_sequence[i]`) can check every reachable puzzle.
//
// Sizing: a 10-minute battle is the longest; even a very fast solver clearing a
// puzzle every ~2s tops out near ~300, but realistic Blitz throughput is far
// lower. 250 is a comfortable floor that will not be exhausted in a time-boxed
// (≤10-min) battle. The deterministic `blitzDiceForIndex` fallback in
// HeadToHeadGame is a defensive backstop for the practically-impossible overflow
// past this length — it keeps both devices synced even then. Was previously
// inconsistent: 75 (CreateChallenge) vs 10 (ChallengeFriend, which diverged at
// puzzle #11). See docs/bugs.md 2026-07-30.
//
// ⚠️ DO NOT SHRINK THIS. A 2026-08-24 design proposed cutting it to 100 (and a
// 2026-09-04 one repeated the idea) on the grounds that the deepest player-side
// in production history was 51, then 61. David ruled against it on 2026-09-04:
// "the deck should never be depleted… so that the players always have puzzles to
// solve and their sequence never diverges and everything is the same for each
// player." Never-depleted is a different bar from minimally-sufficient, and 250
// is the one already shipped. EXPERT MATCHES 250 — an Expert deck shorter than
// the Easy and Challenge decks would be a regression, not a saving.
//
// The cost consequence is the whole reason Expert needs a PRE-BANKED pool rather
// than per-battle vetting: at a measured ~1.4–2.6 s per accepted Expert roll, a
// 250-roll deck is ~350–650 s of CPU, against an edge runtime already observed to
// die at 3.3–5.4 s with HTTP 546 WORKER_RESOURCE_LIMIT. Sizing up made the pool
// more necessary, not less.
export const BATTLE_DICE_SEQUENCE_LENGTH = 250;

// Selectable battle lengths (M3). `seconds` is the stored `time_limit_seconds`.
export const BATTLE_DURATION_OPTIONS = [
  { minutes: 3, seconds: 180, label: '3 min' },
  { minutes: 5, seconds: 300, label: '5 min' },
  { minutes: 7, seconds: 420, label: '7 min' },
  { minutes: 10, seconds: 600, label: '10 min' },
];

// Colosseum "Battles" slide tagline. Kept duration-agnostic now that battle
// length is selectable (3–10 min) — replaces the stale hardcoded "10-minute
// speed races with friends" that predated the duration picker (Bryce, 1.0.285).
export const BATTLE_LENGTH_TAGLINE = '3–10 minute speed races with friends';
