import React from 'react';

// badgeIcons — the cohesive SVG art set for the badge catalog (Badges Phase 2,
// Slice 5). It replaces the one-emoji-per-badge placeholder the registry shipped
// with: emoji render as a different picture on every platform (Android's ➗ and
// iOS's ➗ are not the same drawing), they carry their own baked-in colours that
// fight the cosmic-neon palette, and half the catalog ended up wearing a stock
// office glyph — a chart, a speaker, a camera — for a feat about mathematics.
//
// THE SYSTEM. Every icon is drawn on the same 24×24 grid in the Lucide geometry
// system — the icon language the rest of the app already speaks (`lucide-react`
// is a direct dependency; the icons whose shapes Lucide already provides are
// traced from it, ISC licensed, © Lucide Contributors). Uniform rules, no
// exceptions:
//
//   * viewBox `0 0 24 24`, no per-icon transform
//   * `fill="none"`, `stroke="currentColor"`, `strokeWidth` 1.75, round caps
//     and joins — set ONCE on the <svg> in `BadgeIcon`, never per path
//   * the only permitted colour value anywhere in this file is `currentColor`.
//     No hex, no `rgb()`, no `var(--u37-*)`. The icon takes the colour of
//     whatever paints it, which is what lets BadgeFrame tint the glyph from the
//     badge's MODE accent without this module knowing modes exist. There is a
//     test that greps the rendered markup for a colour literal and fails on one.
//   * solid accents (dots, flag squares, medal numerals) opt in with
//     `fill="currentColor" stroke="none"` — still currentColor, still inherited.
//
// The set is VENDORED as inline JSX rather than imported per-icon: ~half the
// catalog needs shapes no general-purpose UI set has (an exponent, a nested
// parenthesis pair, all-four-operations, a podium, a gladiator helm), and a
// half-Lucide/half-custom import list is exactly how an icon set stops looking
// like one set. Drawing them all here on one grid is what makes the showcase
// read as a single family.
//
// WHERE THE KEYS COME FROM. `Badge.icon` in src/game-mechanics/badgeRegistry.js
// is the key into `ICONS`. `Badge.art` (the emoji) survives in the registry as a
// documented fallback, but as of Slice 7 NOTHING reads it: the last consumer was
// the celebration pop-up in src/game-flow/awards.js, which popped an emoji over
// the very card that had just drawn the badge as an SVG. It now resolves through
// `badgeIconKey` like every other surface. Retiring the field outright is a
// follow-up (the registry was being edited concurrently).

/**
 * The icon set. Key → the <svg> children for that concept.
 *
 * Grouped in catalog order (craft → on-ramp → dojo → arena → solo →
 * cross-arena → the folded-in existing systems), then the two reserved keys.
 * (Round 2 § D3 removed the `firsts` group that sat between dojo and arena;
 * those badges' icons moved into the group for the mode that earns them.)
 *
 * @type {Record<string, React.ReactElement>}
 */
const ICONS = {
